delivery-guard — the guard's own failure states, constructed

  ✓ no-observation: a guard that never ran does NOT report
  ✓ no-observation: and it names that condition specifically
  ✓ no-observation: its detail says this is not a statement about the daemon
  ✓ a CURRENT reading of a DEAD daemon still REPORTS — the guard can say the daemon is down
  ✓ and that report carries serving:false, so 'reporting' is never mistaken for 'healthy'
  ✓ and it carries the age of the observation it is reporting
  ✓ guard-stale: a reading past the freshness bound does NOT report as current
  ✓ guard-stale: and it names that condition specifically
  ✓ guard-stale: the stale reading was SERVING, and it is STILL refused — the guard's own silence outranks a good last look
  ✓ guard-stale: it carries the age and the bound it exceeded, not just the fact that it did
  ✓ guard-stale inverse control: exactly AT the bound still reports — the refusal is bounded, not universal
  ✓ guard-clock-fault: a backwards clock does NOT report
  ✓ guard-clock-fault: and it names that condition rather than lease-stale or no-observation
  ✓ guard-clock-fault: THE AGE IS NOT CLAMPED TO ZERO — a clamp would render stale evidence as fresh
  ✓ a health read that CANNOT COMPLETE still yields an observation, not an absence
  ✓ and that observation is a NAMED refusal, not a swallowed error
  ✓ and it carries the underlying detail rather than discarding it
  ✓ observeOnce inverse control: a completing check yields the health it produced
  ✓ every state renders a non-empty line
  ✓ the property set is NON-EMPTY, so the .every assertions above are not vacuous
  ✓ NO rendering contains a bare 'unknown' — a reader takes unknown for fine
  ✓ every NON-reporting state renders a line saying health was NOT established
  ✓ no NON-reporting state renders as if it were current
  ✓ the stale render SHOWS its held reading but marks it NOT current
  ✓ every REPORTING state leads with the age of its observation

